00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.610
Now back to our chatbots.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:54.129
Let’s get a thing out of the way: How do
chatbots like ChatGPT work?

00:02:54.129 --> 00:03:02.080
They are based on language models, which is
just a fancy neural network that can do autocomplete.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.080
Language models predict next probable words
given previous words.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.379
Even before the user starts conversing with
chatbots like Sparrow or ChatGPT, they already

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:19.739
have an input, the so-called prompt, which
is basically a description of the conversational

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:22.510
persona given by the programmers.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:27.640
This prompt increases the probability that
the next words the language model will spit

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.019
out, are in line with this persona.

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:35.769
So, if here in the prompt we have the word
“helpful”, then most likely, the model

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:42.320
will predict next words that match this description
and will not start using swear words – as

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.320
easily.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.020
Ok, so how does this language model converse?

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:52.150
After this input prompt, which is hidden from
you in ChatGPT’s interface, it is your turn

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:58.760
to ask it a question, then given your question
and the persona description, it delivers an

00:03:58.760 --> 00:03:59.760
answer.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.010
Then you say something again and it reads
the prompt, your first question, its answer,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.180
your second question and based on this, it
continues to say likely words that come after

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.930
this whole history.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:17.030
And this goes on and on, which each interaction
from your side and the model’s response,

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.760
you make the model’s input, longer and longer.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.971
The “magic” thing about these large language
models behind ChatGPT, is that they are super

00:04:25.971 --> 00:04:29.690
fancy autocompletes that can pick patterns.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:35.060
For instance, if you prompt them with descriptions
and examples that tell them about an HTML

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.410
alternative, called HBML, they start to pick
up the pattern,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:44.580
and the most likely words following your examples
are the correct solution to the things you

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.650
just entered.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.310
This is called in-context few-shot learning.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.630
In-context, because it is not like the model’s
parameters update through learning in the

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:00.380
classical sense of weight optimization, it
is just that the input already has a pattern

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:05.850
in it that elicits correct answers from the
model, making it seem like it has learned

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:06.930
something.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:11.639
That the model itself does not update, becomes
clear when we see that this whole history

00:05:11.639 --> 00:05:18.830
of conversation and all things the model “learned”
from you is gone when you or somebody else

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:24.720
opens another conversation with the chatbot,
the input is now gone and the model works

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.300
just with the prompt and not with the things
you had previously conversed with it, or other

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.910
people did.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:35.169
In this new conversation, the history starts
anew, so it is not like the model learns anything

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:39.460
from past sessions of conversations or from
other users.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:45.990
Now we maybe want to know how ChatGPT from
OpenAI works.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:51.699
ChatGPT itself does not have a paper explaining
it, but it has a blog post.

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:59.199
Here we find out that it is a sibling of InstructGPT
and it was trained similarly to InstructGPT

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:05.460
which has a paper from which we find out that
it works very much like Sparrow from DeepMind.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.930
And in this video, we would rather present
the Sparrow paper since it came later and

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.530
is a bit more comprehensive, you’ll see
why.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.200
At the end of the video, we will highlight
the differences between Sparrow and InstructGPT,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.139
so therefore as much as we know about ChatGPT
so far.

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:26.770
[Sparrow explained] So, Sparrow is DeepMind’s
version for a chatbot.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:32.490
Interestingly, the Sparrow paper never uses
the word “chatbot” and maybe it has something

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:37.590
to do with the fact that for a long while,
it was out of fashion to use the term, since

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:43.860
research in chatbots was hyped starting with
Eliza in the 1960s and it failed to deliver

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.550
onto its promises.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:53.080
So, research building chatbots, such as DeepMind
here with Sparrow, preferred to use a other

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.520
terms like “dialogue agent”, “dialogue
system” or “conversational AI”.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:02.560
It is relieving to see that OpenAI makes the
word chatbot fashionable again by naming their

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:09.150
model “ChatGPT” and look, they even use
the word “chatbot” in their blog post

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.330
once!

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:17.160
We think it is a pity DeepMind did not release
Sparrow or at least gave access to users to

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:23.099
interact with the model, like OpenAI does
with ChatGPT, especially since we find the

00:07:23.099 --> 00:07:27.850
Sparrow method, its motivation and paper really
cool.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:33.510
Why did this team of researchers think about
building Sparrow?

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:38.770
Their motivation was that language models
when prompted to behave like chatbots tend

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:46.490
to say inappropriate things, including sexist,
or racist statements and tend to be opinionated.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:51.950
The language model just doing its thing of
predicting next probable words to what was

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:57.409
just being said, does not have even the means
of knowing whether it is offending somebody.

00:07:57.409 --> 00:08:03.819
So, the idea with Sparrow is,
what if we could define a set of rules and

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:07.160
finetune the language model to follow the
rules.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:13.320
While this sounds easy, it is quite hard to
convince a language model to follow rules

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:20.150
and the author’s solution here involves
human feedback at a smaller scale about the

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:29.300
model following or breaking rules, then training
classifiers that will mimic human feedback

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:36.470
at larger scale and then fine-tuning the language
model with reinforcement learning to follow

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:39.260
the feedback of the classifiers.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:44.760
And since the classifiers reflect the human
feedback and the human feedback tells us something

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.890
about the model following the rules or not,
we should have convinced the language model

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:52.740
to follow rules.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:56.270
This seems complicated, how to do this more
exactly?

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:04.940
The authors build Sparrow by fine-tuning Chinchilla,
their 70 billion parameter language model

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:11.079
that already has great capabilities at producing
high quality text and solving natural language

00:09:11.079 --> 00:09:12.839
understanding tasks.

00:09:12.839 --> 00:09:19.190
To make it a chatbot, or “dialogue agent”,
the authors use this prompt to convince it

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:26.720
to take the conversational persona of a “highly
knowledgeable and intelligent AI assistant”,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:34.190
give it its name (“Sparrow”) and describe
what the user should expect from it and also

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:36.079
an example conversation.

00:09:36.079 --> 00:09:41.079
Now, following this prompt, a user can type
in a question or another conversation starter

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:46.290
and Chinchilla will take in the prompt, the
user’s question and then give the answer.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:51.310
But Sparrow can do something more than just
converse, which is provide evidence for the

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.300
answers it gives.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.810
To achieve this, the authors do a specific
update to Sparrow’s prompt:

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:04.269
we have two more participants in the conversation,
namely the Search Query and the Search results.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:09.490
The “Search Query” is basically also a
Sparrow persona, where the language model

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:14.959
generates a search query based on what was
said in the conversation so far.

00:10:14.959 --> 00:10:20.700
And the “Search Results” is basically
a call to Google Search and provides a short

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.490
preview and link to the result.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:29.100
So now this whole thing containing the description
for Sparrow, example User inputs and Search

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:35.310
Queries and Search Results are the prompt
which Sparrow always has as input and generates

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.459
answers with.

00:10:37.459 --> 00:10:42.230
Now we come to the part where the authors
make Sparrow also follow rules.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:46.070
First, the authors come up with 23 rules.

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:52.350
The rules roughly cover 3 categories for making
Sparrow more helpful, correct and harmless.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:56.899
Of course, this list is not exhaustive, as
the authors themselves admit.

00:10:56.899 --> 00:11:01.550
One could (and should) come up with more rules
and apply the whole strategy we are describing

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:03.649
here on the additional rules.

00:11:03.649 --> 00:11:09.820
Now, to make Sparrow follow these rules, the
authors let it talk without it knowing about

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:14.810
these rules and humans annotate how well it
does, on different aspects: they tell which

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:19.810
rules are violated, and whether it should
search the internet to provide evidence and

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:24.290
in case it provided evidence, whether it was
good.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:28.750
Humans are also given an adversarial setting
where they try their best to break the model

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.920
and make it violate the rules.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:35.110
Here, in a sense, the humans are encouraged
to get the worst from the model.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:40.210
This is valuable training data for fine-tuning
Sparrow, since the new iteration of the model

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:43.740
should avoid the worst of the last iteration.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:50.029
So this is the human annotation stage, where
humans give feedback on how Sparrow is performing.

00:11:50.029 --> 00:11:54.520
Since Sparrow at this stage is just a Chinchilla
language model prompted for conversation,

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.530
it should not do that good.

00:11:56.530 --> 00:12:02.209
Now, the authors take the human generated
annotations to train a neural network to classify

00:12:02.209 --> 00:12:07.580
how probable it is for a human to like the
answer Sparrow produced.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:14.470
They train another classifier to say whether
a response is likely to break a rule or not.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:20.180
These classifiers are useful, because now
the authors can train Sparrow with them.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:25.790
For each answer Sparrow produces during training,
a classifier can extremely quickly estimate

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.130
the human feedback for it.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:36.060
Asking humans during training for so many
examples, would make training last forever.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:44.540
So, the authors froze the last 64 layers of
Chinchilla and fine-tuned the last 16 layers

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:50.370
with reinforcement learning taking the output
scores of the classifiers, enforcing the model

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:57.150
to have low scores on rule violation and high
scores for the estimate of a human to appreciate

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:58.250
the answer.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:03.510
For training, they used a dataset consisting
of questions and answers from the Explain

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:09.730
me Like I’m 5 dataset, also conversations
from the human annotators and the generations

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:14.949
of Sparrow’s last iteration, because why
not apply this strategy in more stages, until

00:13:14.949 --> 00:13:20.310
Sparrow becomes better and better at following
rules and searching the Internet to provide

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.020
evidence?

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:27.420
During inference with the last iteration of
Sparrow, to get the best answer from the model,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:31.980
the authors let Sparrow generate multiple
answers and give to the user the answer that

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.889
ranks best given the outputs of the classifiers.

00:13:35.889 --> 00:13:42.320
Ok, how much better did Chinchilla get after
all this heavy lifting involving human annotation

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.670
and reinforcement learning to become Sparrow?

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:52.750
Humans say that Chinchilla delivers answers
that are plausible 61% of the time, while

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:56.850
Sparrow’s answers are plausible and supported
by evidence 78% of the time.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.269
This is looks good.

00:13:59.269 --> 00:14:04.660
Chinchilla breaks rules 20% of the time, while
Sparrow only 8% of the time.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:10.139
But what I love about this Sparrow paper is
the extensive evaluation of the models.

00:14:10.139 --> 00:14:15.970
Surprisingly, fine-tuning Chinchilla to become
Sparrow, reinforced some of the language model’s

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:17.130
stereotypes.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:21.610
Just to give you one example, the scores of
Sparrow are worse on the Winogender dataset

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:26.839
measuring bias towards gender, than for the
baseline Chinchilla model.

00:14:26.839 --> 00:14:32.329
Now that we know about Sparrow, let’s highlight
its differences to ChatGPT.

00:14:32.329 --> 00:14:38.149
First, Sparrow was prompted and trained to
deliver evidence for its answers when needed,

00:14:38.149 --> 00:14:42.070
by searching the Internet and providing the
user with the link.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:44.190
ChatGPT cannot do that.

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:51.040
ChatGPT is also a prompted language model
based on GPT 3.5, it was also trained through

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:54.500
fine-tuning on conversations provided by humans.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:59.250
So, humans rank the language model’s responses
and this data is used to train a classifier

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:06.290
modelling how a human would rank ChatGPT’s
answer, much like with Sparrow.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:10.180
Fine-tuning with reinforcement learning uses
the information provided by the model for

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:15.230
estimating a human in the loop and therefore
trains to provide better answers.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:21.649
But ChatGPT misses one very interesting component
that Sparrow has: Sparrow also had training

00:15:21.649 --> 00:15:27.000
from a classifier trained on human annotation
telling it how well it can follow a set of

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.000
rules.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:33.670
So, in a sense, ChatGPT is like Sparrow, but
without the capability of searching for evidence

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.900
and without rule following, which makes it
a bit underwhelming.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:45.000
Hey, Ms. Coffee Bean, maybe Sparrow’s conception
was more far-sighted than ChatGPTs, but we

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:49.160
at least have access to ChatGPT and can play
with it.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:54.380
Regarding Sparrow, we can only look at the
paper and dream to play with the model.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.029
What a world!

00:15:56.029 --> 00:16:01.699
We have models without detailed papers and
we have detailed papers without model releases.

00:16:01.699 --> 00:16:07.959
Should we even start dreaming about model
weights and code releases?

00:16:07.959 --> 00:16:13.529
We hope you enjoyed our overview about chatbots
these days and we hope to see you next time!

00:16:13.529 --> 00:16:14.430
Okay, bye!

